+++
title: Thoth
description: Ancient Egyptian deity of the Moon, learning, writing
hook: The scribes who wrote ancient Egypt down kept a picture of Thoth in
  their offices, and buried millions of mummified ibises in his honour. He was
  the god who gave them writing — and, in Plato's telling, the god a king told
  that his gift was a remedy for reminding, not remembering.
standfirst: Ibis-headed god of the Moon, writing, wisdom and judgement,
  worshipped at Hermopolis. He recorded the verdict when hearts were weighed,
  won five days out of the Moon at gambling, ended up as the Greeks' Hermes,
  and had the value of his own gift of writing doubted by Plato.
key-facts:
  - name: Where he was worshipped
    value: His major cult center was Hermopolis
  - name: How you know him in a picture
    value: Ibis, moon disk, papyrus scroll, reed pens, writing palette, stylus,
      baboon, scales
  - name: Consorts
    value: Maat, Seshat, Nehmetawy
  - name: Offspring
    value: Seshat
  - name: The Greeks called him
    value: Hermes
  - name: The Romans called him
    value: Mercury
pull-quotes:
  - text: I am thy writing palette, O Thoth, and I have brought unto thee thine
      ink-jar. I am not of those who work iniquity in their secret places; let
      not evil happen unto me.
    attribution: The scribe, in the Papyrus of Ani copy of the Egyptian Book of the Dead
    section: history
  - text: Thoth is a protection over me
    attribution: Egyptian hieroglyphs on an Achaemenid cylinder seal from Iran, c.
      6th–5th century BC
    section: history
timeline:
  - when: c. 6th–5th century BC
    what: An Achaemenid cylinder seal from Iran is cut with Egyptian hieroglyphs
      reading "Thoth is a protection over me".
  - when: third or second century BC
    what: Artapanus of Alexandria euhemerizes Thoth-Hermes as a historical human
      being and claims he was the same person as Moses.
  - when: "1826"
    what: The very large pronaos of the Temple of Thoth at Hermopolis is still
      standing.
  - when: mid-19th century
    what: The pronaos is demolished and used as fill for the foundation of a sugar
      factory.
  - when: c. 1904
    what: An image of Thoth appears in The Gods of the Egyptians Volume 1 by E. A.
      Wallis Budge.
  - when: January 2020
    what: Egypt's Minister of Tourism and Antiquities announces the discovery of
      the collective graves of senior officials and high clergies of Thoth at
      Tuna el-Gebel.
