    role: hero
    section: lead
    caption: "Thoth gives the ankh, the Egyptian symbol for life, to pharaoh Seti
      I: a relief in the Temple of Seti I at Abydos."
  - file: Thoth_as_baboo_E17496.jpg
    role: inline
    section: depictions
    caption: "Not always a bird: a faience statue of Thoth as a baboon, with the
      moon-disk on his head."
  - file: Tot,_Egyptian_God.svg
    role: inline
    section: attributes
    caption: Thoth as drawn for The Gods of the Egyptians Volume 1 by E. A. Wallis
      Budge, c. 1904.
  - file: The_Weighing_of_the_Heart.svg
    role: full
    section: history
    caption: The jackal-headed Anubis weighs a heart against the feather of truth
      on the scale of Maat while the ibis-headed Thoth records the result. A
      heart equal to the feather passes to the afterlife; an imbalance is a
      meal for Ammit, the chimera of crocodile, lion, and hippopotamus.
      Details based on the Papyrus of Ani.
  - file: Stela_showing_a_male_adorer_standing_before_2_Ibises_of_Thoth._Limestone,_sunken_relief._Early_19th_Dynasty._From_Egypt._The_Petrie_Museum_of_Egyptian_Archaeology,_London.jpg
    role: inline
    section: history
    caption: A worshipper stands before two ibises of Thoth on a limestone stela
      in sunken relief, early 19th Dynasty.
  - file: Cylinder_seal_and_modern_impression_king_holding_two_lion_griffins_at_bay_and_Egyptian_hieroglyphs_ca._6th–5th_century_BC.jpg
    role: inline
    section: history
    caption: 'Thoth travelled: a modern impression of an Achaemenid cylinder seal
      from Iran, c. 6th–5th century BC, showing a king holding two lion
      griffins at bay beside Egyptian hieroglyphs reading "Thoth is a
      protection over me".'
  - file: Thoth_writing.svg
    role: inline
    section: modern-cultural-references
    caption: Thoth with the tools of his trade, a book and a stylus.
glossary:
  - term: Ibis
    gloss: The bird sacred to Thoth and the usual head on his human body; possibly
      because the Egyptians saw the curve of its beak as a symbol of the
      crescent moon.
  - term: Hermopolis
    gloss: Thoth's chief temple city — Egyptian //ḫmnw//, Khemenu, Coptic Shmun,
      later el-Ashmunein — named by the Greeks as the //city of Hermes//.
  - term: Ma'at
    gloss: Truth and order, and the goddess of it; the principle represented by
      the feather weighed against a dead person's heart.
  - term: Duat
    gloss: The underworld, where Thoth appeared as the ape Aani, god of equilibrium.
  - term: Ankh
    gloss: The Egyptian symbol for life, which Thoth is often depicted holding.
  - term: Hermes Trismegistus
    gloss: The figure Thoth and Hermes were eventually combined into;
      "Trismegistus" is the Greek τρισμέγιστος, a translation of Thoth's title
      "Thrice great".
  - term: Euhemerize
    gloss: To treat a god as a historical human being, as Artapanus of Alexandria
      did with Thoth-Hermes.
  - term: Pschent
    gloss: The double crown of Upper and Lower Egypt, one of the crowns Thoth is
      sometimes shown wearing.
further:
  - slug: book-of-the-dead
    why: The Papyrus of Ani copy carries the scribe's prayer to Thoth, and Plate
      XXIX Chapter CLXXV is said to be the oldest tradition written by Thoth
      himself.
  - slug: duat
    why: The underworld where Thoth appeared as the ape Aani and reported when the
      scales holding heart and feather came out exactly even.
  - slug: artapanus-of-alexandria
    why: The Egyptian Jew of the third or second century BC who made Thoth-Hermes
      a man, and that man Moses.
